{"ratio":0.83,"watchRatio":0.4861},{"ratio":0.84,"watchRatio":0.48450000000000004},{"ratio":0.85,"watchRatio":0.48119999999999996},{"ratio":0.86,"watchRatio":0.47950000000000004},{"ratio":0.87,"watchRatio":0.4765},{"ratio":0.88,"watchRatio":0.4693},{"ratio":0.89,"watchRatio":0.4664},{"ratio":0.9,"watchRatio":0.4646},{"ratio":0.91,"watchRatio":0.4585},{"ratio":0.92,"watchRatio":0.4531},{"ratio":0.93,"watchRatio":0.44799999999999995},{"ratio":0.94,"watchRatio":0.4457},{"ratio":0.95,"watchRatio":0.4391},{"ratio":0.96,"watchRatio":0.4322},{"ratio":0.97,"watchRatio":0.4233},{"ratio":0.98,"watchRatio":0.41100000000000003},{"ratio":0.99,"watchRatio":0.3973},{"ratio":1,"watchRatio":0.3918}],"script":{"source_path":"all-146-tagged.json","words":177,"wpm":null,"lang":"en","chapters":[],"blocks":[],"text":"Vanquisher of Sultans. This is how faced its long-time rival, the Adil Shahis of Bijapur. Spies brought word that the Sultan had openly insulted KDR. That was enough. He marched toward the Krishna River, the traditional boundary between the two kingdoms. But when he arrived, he found something unexpected. The combined forces of Bijapur, Ahmednagar, and Golconda had already crossed the river. They were camped inside Vijayanagara territory preparing for what they called an annual jihad. [music] The enemy was large, confident, and united. KDR had a plan. Before the battle began, he sent a trusted commander into the enemy camp at night to cut down the Sultan's royal battle flag. Confusion spread through the enemy ranks. KDR gave the signal to attack. The allied army broke ranks and rushed toward the flooded Krishna River. Many drowned in the retreat. KDR did not stop at the river. He pursued them deep into Bijapur territory. In the campaign that followed, Yusuf Adil Shah was killed by KDR. And from that victory, he earned the name history still remembers."}}